// @test: verify-pass
// @tier: 3
// @level: L3
// @tags: proofs, refinement, ensures, reflection, assignment, mutable-local
// @timeout: 30000

/// A function that ASSIGNS to a mutable local can be verified.
///
/// Until this spec existed the verifier could not discharge a postcondition
/// for any imperative body. Measured on one binary, `ensures result == 1`:
///
///     { let acc = 1; acc }                    PROVED
///     { let mut acc = 1; acc }                PROVED
///     { let mut acc = 0; acc = 1; acc }       FAILED   <- one statement more
///     { let mut acc = 0; acc = acc + 1; acc } FAILED
///
/// Two independent places read the body and both stopped at the assignment.
/// Reflection declined the whole function — "body has a statement that is
/// neither a `let` nor an early return" — so a call to it unfolded to
/// nothing. And the function's OWN postcondition was checked by binding
/// `result` to the block's BARE TAIL while the statement walk had asserted
/// `acc == 0` from the `let` and nothing at all about the assignment. The
/// solver was handed a body arguing against its own contract and returned
/// the counterexample `acc = 0, result = 0`.
///
/// The rule is that an assignment to a local is a REBINDING: `acc = acc + 1`
/// means the same as introducing a fresh `acc` bound to the old one plus
/// one, which the substitution model already handled. Resolving the
/// right-hand side against the CURRENT bindings before replacing the old
/// entry is what makes that equivalence hold — the stored value closes over
/// the state the assignment saw.
///
/// The soundness pole is `mutable_local_rebinding_soundness.vr`: the same
/// bodies with FALSE postconditions, which must still fail. A fold that
/// simply dropped assignments would satisfy this file and be caught there.
///
/// Loops are deliberately absent. Every undischarged obligation in the
/// showcase application blamed a loop, and the loops were the loudest
/// symptom of this defect rather than its cause; a body with a loop is
/// still HAVOC'd without a user invariant, which is a separate subject.
///
/// Task: T0954.

@verify(formal)
public fn assign_then_read(n: Int) -> Int
    requires n == 0
    ensures result == 1
{
    let mut acc = 0;
    acc = 1;
    acc
}

@verify(formal)
public fn assignment_reads_its_own_old_value(n: Int) -> Int
    requires n == 0
    ensures result == 1
{
    let mut acc = 0;
    acc = acc + 1;
    acc
}

@verify(formal)
public fn repeated_assignment_accumulates(n: Int) -> Int
    requires n == 0
    ensures result == 4
{
    let mut acc = 0;
    acc = acc + 1;
    acc = acc + 1;
    acc = acc + 1;
    acc = acc + 1;
    acc
}

/// The classic swap. Any fold that stored right-hand sides UNRESOLVED, or
/// applied its substitutions in the wrong order, gets a different answer
/// here while still agreeing on every function above.
@verify(formal)
public fn swap_through_a_temporary(n: Int) -> Int
    requires n == 0
    ensures result == 1
{
    let mut a = 1;
    let mut b = 2;
    let t = a;
    a = b;
    b = t;
    a - b
}

mount core.prelude.*;

fn main() {
    print("mutable locals verify");
}
